# Target C-II — a common diameter-routing hub makes every nonhub adjacent switch one-sided

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only exact structural theorem  
**Scope:** any laminar O--O dual path whose selected support path for every actual diameter pair contains one common dual region; live application supplied by `target_c_opposite_branch_dual_path_t18593_2026-09-15.md`  
**Dependencies:** exact laminar interval support; diameter-path Helly hub; adjacent strip/switching theorems  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let the selected path be

\[
R_0\xleftrightarrow{\tau_1}R_1
\xleftrightarrow{\tau_2}\cdots
\xleftrightarrow{\tau_m}R_m,
\]

and suppose one region

\[
R_*=R_k
\]

lies on the endpoint-region path of every actual diameter pair.

For a diameter pair `h`, orient its endpoint regions by increasing path index,

\[
R_{a_h},R_{b_h},
\qquad a_h<b_h.
\]

Exact laminar interval support gives

\[
I_h=\{a_h+1,\ldots,b_h\},
\]

while the hub property is

\[
\boxed{a_h\le k\le b_h\qquad(h\in H).}
\tag{HS.1}
\]

Consequently:

1. at every internal boundary `i<k`, switching rows can only **start** there;
2. at every internal boundary `i>k`, switching rows can only **end** there;
3. only the single hub boundary `i=k` can contain both switch orientations;
4. active diameter sets are nested toward the hub on each side; and
5. normalized KKT crossing mass is monotone toward the hub on each side.

These conclusions are exact and conditional only on the laminar path plus common-hub hypotheses.  They do not depend on the quantitative high-`t` extraction.

The currently valid live application is the repaired opposite-branch theorem through

\[
1.844<t\le1.8593.
\]

It supplies

\[
m>0.0003D-o(D)
\]

selected path cuts.  After discarding the single hub boundary, one nonhub adjacent strip has at most

\[
\boxed{20943}
\]

moving actual diameter rows, and **every one of them joins the middle layer toward the hub side**.  The exact nonzero KKT layer reaction is generated by one or two bars from that same one-sided switching set.

The former numerical application `t<=1.855` with `m>0.0184D` and `N_i<=341` depended on a retracted path theorem whose branch-orientation step was incomplete.  Those numerical claims are withdrawn; the exact one-sided-switch theorem itself is unchanged.

---

## 1. Switching is one-sided away from the hub

Fix the internal boundary between selected cuts `tau_i,tau_{i+1}`.  Its unique middle layer is `R_i`.

A diameter row meeting cut `i` but not `i+1` has interval right endpoint

\[
b_h=i.
\]

A diameter row meeting cut `i+1` but not `i` has interval left endpoint

\[
a_h=i.
\]

If `i<k`, the first possibility contradicts (HS.1).  Therefore every switch at `i<k` is a start, and every switching edge joins `R_i` to a region on the hub side.

If `i>k`, the second possibility contradicts (HS.1).  Therefore every switch at `i>k` is an end, again joining `R_i` toward the hub side.

At `i=k`, both possibilities are compatible with (HS.1), so this is the unique potentially mixed boundary.

---

## 2. Active diameter sets and KKT mass are nested toward the hub

Let

\[
\mathcal H_j:=\{h:j\in I_h\}.
\]

For `j<k`, no interval can end at boundary `j`, hence

\[
\boxed{\mathcal H_j\subseteq\mathcal H_{j+1}.}
\tag{HS.2}
\]

For `j>k`, no interval can start at boundary `j`, hence

\[
\boxed{\mathcal H_j\supseteq\mathcal H_{j+1}.}
\tag{HS.3}
\]

With positive diameter KKT multipliers normalized by

\[
\sum_h\beta_h=1,
\]

put

\[
w_j:=\sum_{h\in\mathcal H_j}\beta_h.
\]

Then

\[
w_j\le w_{j+1}\quad(j<k),
\qquad
w_j\ge w_{j+1}\quad(j>k).
\tag{HS.4}
\]

No individual multiplier floor is used.

---

## 3. Corrected live finite-strip consequence

The repaired high-`t` path theorem gives more than `0.0003D-o(D)` selected cuts through `t<=1.8593`.  Let `N_i` be the number of diameter intervals which start or stop at internal boundary `i`.

Every actual diameter interval has at most one start and one end, so over all nonhub boundaries

\[
\sum_{i\ne k}N_i\le2|H|\le2\pi D.
\]

The number of nonhub boundaries remains

\[
0.0003D-o(D).
\]

Using `pi<355/113`,

\[
{2(355/113)\over0.0003}
=20943.9528\ldots<20944.
\]

Therefore some nonhub adjacent boundary satisfies

\[
\boxed{N_i\le20943.}
\tag{HS.5}
\]

By the one-sided theorem, all these moving diameter edges point topologically toward the hub side of the middle layer.

The adjacent-strip translation theorem makes every nonswitching source-pair distance invariant exactly and opens only the two boundary train contact families.  The strip-reaction nondegeneracy theorem gives a nonzero exact reaction

\[
G_i=\sum_{h\in H_{sw,i}}\beta_h n_h\ne0,
\]

and planar conic Caratheodory compresses `G_i` to one or two actual switching diameter vectors.  Those bars inherit the same one-sided layer-to-hub geometry.

---

## 4. Frontier effect

The valid live normal form is now

```text
repaired opposite-branch O-O dual path through t<=1.8593
+ every actual diameter interval contains one common hub region
+ active diameter sets nested toward the hub
+ only the hub boundary can have mixed switch orientations
+ one nonhub adjacent strip has <=20943 moving diameter rows
+ all moving rows join the layer toward the hub side
+ exact nonzero layer reaction generated by <=2 of those bars.
```

The theorem remains useful independently of the present quantitative extraction: any future longer laminar path with the same common-hub property immediately inherits the same one-sided switching and monotone KKT-mass conclusions.

**Disposition:** exact conditional theorem retained; former unsupported `<=341` live application withdrawn and replaced by the valid `<=20943` application through `t<=1.8593`. C-II and Level I remain open.